    "id": "news-1790410013187-0",
    "title": "Somaliland President Proposes Renaming Airport After Trump in Push for US Recognition",
    "original_title": "Somaliland offers to rename Berbera Airport after Trump",
    "slug": "somaliland-president-proposes-renaming-airport-after-trump-in-push-for-us-recognition",
    "summary": "Somaliland's President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi Irro has suggested renaming Berbera International Airport after former US President Donald Trump, as part of efforts to gain international recognition for the region's independence.",
    "content": "In an interview with the Council on Foreign Relations, Somaliland President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi Irro proposed renaming Berbera International Airport after former US President Donald Trump. The suggestion is part of Somaliland's ongoing campaign for international recognition as an independent state, which it declared in 1991 but has not received widespread acknowledgment.\n\nIrro's proposal follows his earlier idea of inviting Trump to build a Trump Tower in Hargeisa, the capital. \"We are not giving anything for free,\" Irro stated. \"We have to offer something. And we expect the other partner to have the same.\" The airport renaming is seen as a gesture to appeal to US interests and secure support for Somaliland's recognition.\n\nSomaliland, a self-declared republic in northwestern Somalia, has functioned as a de facto independent state for over three decades, maintaining its own government, currency, and security forces. However, it has not received formal recognition from any country, largely due to opposition from Somalia and concerns over regional stability. The United States has historically supported Somalia's territorial integrity while engaging with Somaliland on limited issues.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development highlights the complex political dynamics between Somaliland and Somalia. Many in the diaspora have ties to both regions and closely follow efforts that could alter the political landscape. Some may view Somaliland's overtures to the US as a pragmatic move to gain leverage, while others may worry about potential repercussions for Somali unity. The diaspora's remittances and investments are significant to both economies, making stability and international standing critical concerns.\n\nThis proposal also underscores the growing role of symbolic gestures in international diplomacy. By attaching Trump's name to a key infrastructure asset, Irro aims to draw attention to Somaliland's strategic location near the Gulf of Aden and its potential as a partner in counterterrorism and trade. However, such a move could complicate relations with Somalia and other allies if perceived as a unilateral step toward recognition.\n\nAs Somaliland continues its lobbying efforts, the response from the US and the international community will be closely watched. For now, the proposal remains a suggestion, but it reflects the lengths to which Somaliland is willing to go to achieve its long-sought goal of statehood. The diaspora, with its deep connections to the region, will be attentive to how this unfolds and what it means for their homeland's future.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somaliland-offers-rename-berbera-airport-trump-us-recognition/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somaliland-offers-rename-berbera-airport-trump-us-recognition/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Politics",
    "published_at": "2026-09-26T06:58:27.000Z",
    "first_seen_at": "2026-09-26T08:06:53.187Z",
    "update_batch": "morning",
    "image_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/8-1-1024x997.jpg",
    "featured": false,
